title: 'pallet-proxy: add try_state invariant checks and sort the Proxies map'

doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    In the new `try_state` hook, several storage invariants for the pallet are checked,
    with consideration for future use as an oracle in pallet fuzzing campaigns. The three
    deposit checks warn by default and become hard errors under a new `fuzzing` feature,
    since a live chain can legally hold a stale deposit or an announcement left behind by
    an account migration, neither of which a campaign can produce.

    The sortedness invariant found live state that breaks it: some `Proxies` entries on the
    Polkadot, Kusama and Westend Asset Hubs are unsorted, so `binary_search` misses a
    delegate that is present, leaving the owner unable to remove that proxy or recover its
    deposit while `add_proxy` adds a duplicate instead of failing with `Duplicate`. The
    pallet now declares a storage version and adds `migrations::MigrateV0ToV1`, which sorts
    each entry and drops exact duplicates. Deposits are left as they are, a deduplicated
    entry pricing below its deposit until `poke_deposit` corrects it.

    `MigrateV0ToV1` is a multi-block migration, one entry per step, so it belongs in
    `pallet_migrations::Config::Migrations` rather than in a runtime's single-block
    migration tuple: the map is large enough on the Asset Hubs (4,455 entries on Polkadot,
    262.2 KiB of proof) that walking it in one block is worth avoiding. It is added to the
    live runtimes in this repository that both use the pallet and run multi-block
    migrations, namely Westend, Asset Hub Westend and People Westend. Collectives Westend
    and Coretime Westend have no multi-block migration runner and so cannot take it yet,
    and the Rococo runtimes are skipped because that testnet is retired. The Polkadot and
    Kusama Asset Hub runtimes live in `polkadot-fellows/runtimes` and can take it once this
    is released.
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    Accounts whose proxy list was stored out of order can add and remove proxies again once
    the migration has walked past their entry. A list that held a duplicate is then one
    entry shorter than the deposit recorded for it, and `poke_deposit` returns the
    difference.

